    checklist: ["Location checked for depth, swing and wakes", "Anchor boat and leader designated", "Approach side, fenders and lines prepared", "Bow, stern and spring control established", "Passenger crossing point identified", "Swimming lookout and head count assigned", "Engines controlled before swimmers enter", "Anchor position and chafe rechecked", "Overnight exhaust and bilge risks reviewed", "Breakaway order agreed before it is needed"],
    failures: ["Building the raft before the anchor boat is settled", "Using too few fenders or no spring lines", "Restarting an engine before every swimmer is aboard", "Waiting too long to separate as weather changes"],
    faq: [
      ["How many boats can safely raft together?", "There is no universal number. The location, anchor system, boat sizes, weather, traffic, crew experience and local rules control the practical limit."],
      ["Should every boat deploy an anchor?", "Not automatically. Multiple anchors can improve holding in some arrangements but can also cross, foul or complicate departure. Use a deliberate plan suited to the location."],
      ["Can a raft-up remain overnight?", "Only when the anchoring, weather, watch plan, exhaust control, lights and local rules support it. An overnight raft requires more discipline than a daytime social stop."],
      ["What is the most useful line between boats?", "Spring lines are essential because they limit fore-and-aft surging. Bow and stern lines alone often let boats saw against each other in wakes."]
    ],
